    dc:modified '2006-09-26T18:12:43'^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:altLabel ''^^xsd:string ;
    skos:prefLabel 'specific_convective_available_potential_energy'@en ;
    skos:definition '\'specific\' means per unit mass. Potential energy is the sum of the gravitational potential energy relative to the geoid and the centripetal potential energy. (The geopotential is the specific potential energy.) Convective(ly) available potential energy is often abbreviated as \'CAPE\'.'@en ;
    skos:notation 'SDN:P07::CFSNA001'^^xsd:string .
<feed> tree:member <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA002/1/> .
<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA002/1/>
    a skos:Concept ;
    dc:isVersionOf <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA002/>;
    dc:modified '2006-09-26T18:12:43'^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:altLabel ''^^xsd:string ;
    skos:prefLabel 'atmosphere_so4_content'@en ;
    skos:definition '\'Content\' indicates a quantity per unit area. The \'atmosphere content\' of a quantity refers to the vertical integral from the surface to the top of the atmosphere. For the content between specified levels in the atmosphere, standard names including content_of_atmosphere_layer are used.'@en ;
    skos:notation 'SDN:P07::CFSNA002'^^xsd:string .
<feed> tree:member <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA004/1/> .
<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA004/1/>
    a skos:Concept ;
    dc:isVersionOf <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA004/>;
    dc:modified '2006-09-26T18:12:43'^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:altLabel ''^^xsd:string ;
    skos:prefLabel 'equivalent_thickness_at_stp_of_atmosphere_o3_content'@en ;
    skos:definition '\'stp\' means standard temperature (0 degC) and pressure (101325 Pa). \'Content\' indicates a quantity per unit area. The \'atmosphere content\' of a quantity refers to the vertical integral from the surface to the top of the atmosphere. For the content between specified levels in the atmosphere, standard names including content_of_atmosphere_layer are used. The equivalent thickness at STP of a particular constituent of the atmosphere is the thickness of the layer that the gas would occupy if it was separated from the other constituents and gathered together at STP.'@en ;
    skos:notation 'SDN:P07::CFSNA004'^^xsd:string .
<feed> tree:member <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA005/1/> .
<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA005/1/>
    a skos:Concept ;
    dc:isVersionOf <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA005/>;
    dc:modified '2006-09-26T18:12:43'^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:altLabel ''^^xsd:string ;
    skos:prefLabel 'longwave_radiance'@en ;
    skos:definition '\'longwave\' means longwave radiation. Radiance is the radiative flux in a particular direction, per unit of solid angle. If radiation is isotropic, the radiance is independent of direction, so the direction should not be specified. If the radiation is directionally dependent, a standard name of upwelling or downwelling radiance should be chosen instead.'@en ;
    skos:notation 'SDN:P07::CFSNA005'^^xsd:string .
<feed> tree:member <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA006/1/> .
<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA006/1/>
    a skos:Concept ;
    dc:isVersionOf <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA006/>;
    dc:modified '2006-09-26T18:12:43'^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:altLabel ''^^xsd:string ;
    skos:prefLabel 'shortwave_radiance'@en ;
    skos:definition '\'shortwave\' means shortwave radiation. Radiance is the radiative flux in a particular direction, per unit of solid angle. If radiation is isotropic, the radiance is independent of direction, so the direction should not be specified. If the radiation is directionally dependent, a standard name of upwelling or downwelling radiance should be chosen instead.'@en ;
    skos:notation 'SDN:P07::CFSNA006'^^xsd:string .
<feed> tree:member <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA007/1/> .
<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA007/1/>
    a skos:Concept ;
    dc:isVersionOf <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA007/>;
    dc:modified '2006-09-26T18:12:43'^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:altLabel ''^^xsd:string ;
    skos:prefLabel 'spectral_radiance'@en ;
    skos:definition '\'spectral\' means per unit wavelength or as a function of wavelength; spectral quantities are sometimes called \'monochromatic\'. Radiation wavelength has standard name radiation_wavelength. Radiance is the radiative flux in a particular direction, per unit of solid angle. If radiation is isotropic, the radiance is independent of direction, so the direction should not be specified. If the radiation is directionally dependent, a standard name of upwelling or downwelling radiance should be chosen instead.'@en ;
    skos:notation 'SDN:P07::CFSNA007'^^xsd:string .
<feed> tree:member <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA008/1/> .
<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA008/1/>
    a skos:Concept ;
    dc:isVersionOf <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA008/>;
    dc:modified '2006-09-26T18:12:43'^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:altLabel ''^^xsd:string ;
    skos:prefLabel 'omega'@en ;
    skos:definition '\'tendency_of_X\' means derivative of X with respect to time. The Lagrangian tendency of a quantity is its rate of change following the motion of the fluid, also called the \'material derivative\' or \'convective derivative\'. The Lagrangian tendency of air pressure, often called \'omega\', plays the role of the upward component of air velocity when air pressure is being used as the vertical coordinate. If the vertical air velocity is upwards, it is negative when expressed as a tendency of air pressure; downwards is positive.'@en ;
    skos:notation 'SDN:P07::CFSNA008'^^xsd:string .
<feed> tree:member <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA009/1/> .
<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA009/1/>
    a skos:Concept ;
    dc:isVersionOf <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA009/>;
    dc:modified '2006-09-26T18:12:43'^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:altLabel ''^^xsd:string ;
    skos:prefLabel 'vertical_air_velocity_expressed_as_tendency_of_pressure'@en ;
    skos:definition '\'tendency_of_X\' means derivative of X with respect to time. The Lagrangian tendency of a quantity is its rate of change following the motion of the fluid, also called the \'material derivative\' or \'convective derivative\'. The Lagrangian tendency of air pressure, often called \'omega\', plays the role of the upward component of air velocity when air pressure is being used as the vertical coordinate. If the vertical air velocity is upwards, it is negative when expressed as a tendency of air pressure; downwards is positive.'@en ;
    skos:notation 'SDN:P07::CFSNA009'^^xsd:string .
<feed> tree:member <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA010/1/> .
<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA010/1/>
    a skos:Concept ;
    dc:isVersionOf <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA010/>;
    dc:modified '2006-09-26T18:12:43'^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:altLabel ''^^xsd:string ;
    skos:prefLabel 'upward_air_velocity_expressed_as_tendency_of_sigma'@en ;
    skos:definition '\'tendency_of_X\' means derivative of X with respect to time. The Lagrangian tendency of a quantity is its rate of change following the motion of the fluid, also called the \'material derivative\' or \'convective derivative\'. The Lagrangian tendency of sigma plays the role of the upward component of air velocity when the atmosphere sigma coordinate (a dimensionless atmosphere vertical coordinate) is being used as the vertical coordinate. If the vertical air velocity is upwards, it is negative when expressed as a tendency of sigma; downwards is positive. See Appendix D of the CF convention for information about dimensionless vertical coordinates.'@en ;
    skos:notation 'SDN:P07::CFSNA010'^^xsd:string .
<feed> tree:member <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA011/1/> .
<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA011/1/>
    a skos:Concept ;
    dc:isVersionOf <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA011/>;
    dc:modified '2006-09-26T18:12:43'^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:altLabel ''^^xsd:string ;
    skos:prefLabel 'vertical_air_velocity_expressed_as_tendency_of_sigma'@en ;
    skos:definition '\'tendency_of_X\' means derivative of X with respect to time. The Lagrangian tendency of a quantity is its rate of change following the motion of the fluid, also called the \'material derivative\' or \'convective derivative\'. The Lagrangian tendency of sigma plays the role of the upward component of air velocity when the atmosphere sigma coordinate (a dimensionless atmosphere vertical coordinate) is being used as the vertical coordinate. If the vertical air velocity is upwards, it is negative when expressed as a tendency of sigma; downwards is positive. See Appendix D of the CF convention for information about dimensionless vertical coordinates.'@en ;
    skos:notation 'SDN:P07::CFSNA011'^^xsd:string .
<feed> tree:member <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA012/1/> .
<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA012/1/>
    a skos:Concept ;
    dc:isVersionOf <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA012/>;
    dc:modified '2006-09-26T18:12:43'^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:altLabel ''^^xsd:string ;
    skos:prefLabel 'mole_fraction_of_o3_in_air'@en ;
    skos:definition 'Mole fraction is used in the construction mole_fraction_of_X_in_Y, where X is a material constituent of Y.'@en ;
    skos:notation 'SDN:P07::CFSNA012'^^xsd:string .
<feed> tree:member <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA013/1/> .
<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA013/1/>
    a skos:Concept ;
    dc:isVersionOf <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA013/>;
    dc:modified '2006-09-26T18:12:43'^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:altLabel ''^^xsd:string ;
    skos:prefLabel 'sea_surface_height'@en ;
    skos:definition 'sea_level means mean sea level, which is close to the geoid in sea areas. \'Sea surface height\' is a time-varying quantity. The standard name for the height of the sea surface above the geoid is sea_surface_height_above_geoid.'@en ;
    skos:notation 'SDN:P07::CFSNA013'^^xsd:string .
<feed> tree:member <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA014/1/> .
<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA014/1/>
    a skos:Concept ;
    dc:isVersionOf <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA014/>;
    dc:modified '2006-09-26T18:12:43'^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:altLabel ''^^xsd:string ;
    skos:prefLabel 'significant_height_of_swell_waves'@en ;
    skos:definition 'Height is the vertical distance above the surface. Swell waves are waves on the ocean surface.'@en ;
    skos:notation 'SDN:P07::CFSNA014'^^xsd:string .
<feed> tree:member <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA015/1/> .
<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA015/1/>
    a skos:Concept ;
    dc:isVersionOf <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA015/>;
    dc:modified '2006-09-26T18:12:43'^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:altLabel ''^^xsd:string ;
    skos:prefLabel 'significant_height_of_wind_and_swell_waves'@en ;
    skos:definition 'Height is the vertical distance above the surface.'@en ;
    skos:notation 'SDN:P07::CFSNA015'^^xsd:string .
<feed> tree:member <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA016/1/> .
<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA016/1/>
    a skos:Concept ;
    dc:isVersionOf <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA016/>;
    dc:modified '2006-09-26T18:12:43'^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:altLabel ''^^xsd:string ;
    skos:prefLabel 'x_sea_water_velocity'@en ;
    skos:definition 'A velocity is a vector quantity. \'x\' indicates a vector component along the grid x-axis, when this is not true longitude, positive with increasing x.'@en ;
    skos:notation 'SDN:P07::CFSNA016'^^xsd:string .
<feed> tree:member <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA017/1/> .
<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA017/1/>
    a skos:Concept ;
    dc:isVersionOf <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA017/>;
    dc:modified '2006-09-26T18:12:43'^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:altLabel ''^^xsd:string ;
    skos:prefLabel 'atmosphere_surface_drag_coefficient_of_heat'@en ;
    skos:definition 'The surface called \'surface\' means the lower boundary of the atmosphere.'@en ;
    skos:notation 'SDN:P07::CFSNA017'^^xsd:string .
<feed> tree:member <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA018/1/> .
<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA018/1/>
    a skos:Concept ;
    dc:isVersionOf <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA018/>;
    dc:modified '2006-09-26T18:12:43'^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:altLabel ''^^xsd:string ;
    skos:prefLabel 'atmosphere_surface_drag_coefficient_of_momentum'@en ;
    skos:definition 'The surface called \'surface\' means the lower boundary of the atmosphere.'@en ;
    skos:notation 'SDN:P07::CFSNA018'^^xsd:string .
<feed> tree:member <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA019/1/> .
<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA019/1/>
    a skos:Concept ;
    dc:isVersionOf <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA019/>;
    dc:modified '2006-09-26T18:12:43'^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:altLabel ''^^xsd:string ;
    skos:prefLabel 'atmosphere_surface_drag_coefficient'@en ;
    skos:definition 'The surface called \'surface\' means the lower boundary of the atmosphere.'@en ;
    skos:notation 'SDN:P07::CFSNA019'^^xsd:string .
<feed> tree:member <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA020/1/> .
<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA020/1/>
    a skos:Concept ;
    dc:isVersionOf <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA020/>;
    dc:modified '2006-09-26T18:12:43'^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:altLabel ''^^xsd:string ;
    skos:prefLabel 'water_evaporation_amount_from_canopy_where_land'@en ;
    skos:definition '\'Amount\' means mass per unit area. \'Water\' means water in all phases. \'Canopy\' means the plant or vegetation canopy. Evaporation is the conversion of liquid or solid into vapor. (The conversion of solid alone into vapor is called \'sublimation\'.)'@en ;
    skos:notation 'SDN:P07::CFSNA020'^^xsd:string .
<feed> tree:member <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA021/1/> .
<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA021/1/>
    a skos:Concept ;
    dc:isVersionOf <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA021/>;
    dc:modified '2006-09-26T18:12:43'^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:altLabel ''^^xsd:string ;
    skos:prefLabel 'grid_eastward_wind'@en ;
    skos:definition '\'x\' indicates a vector component along the grid x-axis, when this is not true longitude, positive with increasing x. Wind is defined as a two-dimensional (horizontal) air velocity vector, with no vertical component. (Vertical motion in the atmosphere has the standard name upward_air_velocity.)'@en ;
    skos:notation 'SDN:P07::CFSNA021'^^xsd:string .
<feed> tree:member <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA022/1/> .
<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA022/1/>
    a skos:Concept ;
    dc:isVersionOf <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA022/>;
    dc:modified '2006-09-26T18:12:43'^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:altLabel ''^^xsd:string ;
    skos:prefLabel 'mass_fraction_of_o3_in_air'@en ;
    skos:definition 'Mass fraction is used in the construction mass_fraction_of_X_in_Y, where X is a material constituent of Y. It means the ratio of the mass of Y to the mass of X (including Y).'@en ;
    skos:notation 'SDN:P07::CFSNA022'^^xsd:string .
<feed> tree:member <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA023/1/> .
<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA023/1/>
    a skos:Concept ;
    dc:isVersionOf <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA023/>;
    dc:modified '2006-09-26T18:12:43'^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:altLabel ''^^xsd:string ;
    skos:prefLabel 'electromagnetic_wavelength'@en ;
    skos:definition 'The radiation wavelength can refer to any electromagnetic wave, such as light, heat radiation and radio waves.'@en ;
    skos:notation 'SDN:P07::CFSNA023'^^xsd:string .
<feed> tree:member <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA024/1/> .
<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA024/1/>
    a skos:Concept ;
    dc:isVersionOf <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA024/>;
    dc:modified '2006-09-26T18:12:43'^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:altLabel ''^^xsd:string ;
    skos:prefLabel 'sea_floor_depth'@en ;
    skos:definition 'The geoid is a surface of constant geopotential with which mean sea level would coincide if the ocean were at rest. (The volume enclosed between the geoid and the sea floor equals the mean volume of water in the ocean.) In an ocean GCM the geoid is the surface of zero depth, or the rigid lid if the model uses that approximation.'@en ;
    skos:notation 'SDN:P07::CFSNA024'^^xsd:string .
<feed> tree:member <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA025/1/> .
<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA025/1/>
    a skos:Concept ;
    dc:isVersionOf <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA025/>;
    dc:modified '2006-09-26T18:12:43'^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:altLabel ''^^xsd:string ;
    skos:prefLabel 'sea_surface_elevation'@en ;
    skos:definition 'The geoid is a surface of constant geopotential with which mean sea level would coincide if the ocean were at rest. (The volume enclosed between the geoid and the sea floor equals the mean volume of water in the ocean.) In an ocean GCM the geoid is the surface of zero depth, or the rigid lid if the model uses that approximation. \'Sea surface height\' is a time-varying quantity. By definition of the geoid, the global average of the time-mean sea surface height (i.e. mean sea level) above the geoid must be zero. The standard name for the height of the sea surface above mean sea level is sea_surface_height_above_sea_level.'@en ;
    skos:notation 'SDN:P07::CFSNA025'^^xsd:string .
<feed> tree:member <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA026/1/> .
<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA026/1/>
    a skos:Concept ;
    dc:isVersionOf <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA026/>;
    dc:modified '2006-09-26T18:12:43'^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:altLabel ''^^xsd:string ;
    skos:prefLabel 'sea_surface_elevation_anomaly'@en ;
    skos:definition 'The geoid is a surface of constant geopotential with which mean sea level would coincide if the ocean were at rest. (The volume enclosed between the geoid and the sea floor equals the mean volume of water in the ocean.) In an ocean GCM the geoid is the surface of zero depth, or the rigid lid if the model uses that approximation. \'Sea surface height\' is a time-varying quantity. By definition of the geoid, the global average of the time-mean sea surface height (i.e. mean sea level) above the geoid must be zero. The standard name for the height of the sea surface above mean sea level is sea_surface_height_above_sea_level.'@en ;
    skos:notation 'SDN:P07::CFSNA026'^^xsd:string .
<feed> tree:member <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA027/1/> .
<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA027/1/>
    a skos:Concept ;
    dc:isVersionOf <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA027/>;
    dc:modified '2006-09-26T18:12:43'^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:altLabel ''^^xsd:string ;
    skos:prefLabel 'swell_wave_period'@en ;
    skos:definition 'A period is an interval of time, or the time-period of an oscillation. Swell waves are waves on the ocean surface.'@en ;
    skos:notation 'SDN:P07::CFSNA027'^^xsd:string .
<feed> tree:member <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA028/1/> .
<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA028/1/>
    a skos:Concept ;
    dc:isVersionOf <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA028/>;
    dc:modified '2006-09-26T18:12:43'^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:altLabel ''^^xsd:string ;
    skos:prefLabel 'direction_of_swell_wave_velocity'@en ;
    skos:definition 'Swell waves are waves on the ocean surface. \'to_direction\' is used in the construction X_to_direction and indicates the direction towards which the velocity vector of X is headed.'@en ;
    skos:notation 'SDN:P07::CFSNA028'^^xsd:string .
<feed> tree:member <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA029/1/> .
<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA029/1/>
    a skos:Concept ;
    dc:isVersionOf <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA029/>;
    dc:modified '2006-09-26T18:12:43'^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:altLabel ''^^xsd:string ;
    skos:prefLabel 'wind_wave_period'@en ;
    skos:definition 'A period is an interval of time, or the time-period of an oscillation. Wind waves are waves on the ocean surface. Wind is defined as a two-dimensional (horizontal) air velocity vector, with no vertical component. (Vertical motion in the atmosphere has the standard name upward_air_velocity.)'@en ;
    skos:notation 'SDN:P07::CFSNA029'^^xsd:string .
<feed> tree:member <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA030/1/> .
<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA030/1/>
    a skos:Concept ;
    dc:isVersionOf <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA030/>;
    dc:modified '2006-09-26T18:12:43'^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:altLabel ''^^xsd:string ;
    skos:prefLabel 'significant_height_of_wind_waves'@en ;
    skos:definition 'Height is the vertical distance above the surface. Wind waves are waves on the ocean surface. Wind is defined as a two-dimensional (horizontal) air velocity vector, with no vertical component. (Vertical motion in the atmosphere has the standard name upward_air_velocity.)'@en ;
    skos:notation 'SDN:P07::CFSNA030'^^xsd:string .
<feed> tree:member <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA031/1/> .
<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA031/1/>
    a skos:Concept ;
    dc:isVersionOf <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA031/>;
    dc:modified '2006-09-26T18:12:43'^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:altLabel ''^^xsd:string ;
    skos:prefLabel 'direction_of_wind_wave_velocity'@en ;
    skos:definition 'Wind waves are waves on the ocean surface. Wind is defined as a two-dimensional (horizontal) air velocity vector, with no vertical component. (Vertical motion in the atmosphere has the standard name upward_air_velocity.) \'to_direction\' is used in the construction X_to_direction and indicates the direction towards which the velocity vector of X is headed.'@en ;
    skos:notation 'SDN:P07::CFSNA031'^^xsd:string .
<feed> tree:member <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA032/1/> .
<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA032/1/>
    a skos:Concept ;
    dc:isVersionOf <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA032/>;
    dc:modified '2006-09-26T18:12:43'^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:altLabel ''^^xsd:string ;
    skos:prefLabel 'y_sea_water_velocity'@en ;
    skos:definition 'A velocity is a vector quantity. \'y\' indicates a vector component along the grid y-axis, when this is not true latitude, positive with increasing y.'@en ;
    skos:notation 'SDN:P07::CFSNA032'^^xsd:string .
<feed> tree:member <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA033/1/> .
<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA033/1/>
    a skos:Concept ;
    dc:isVersionOf <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA033/>;
    dc:modified '2006-09-26T18:12:43'^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:altLabel ''^^xsd:string ;
    skos:prefLabel 'specific_potential_energy'@en ;
    skos:definition '\'specific\' means per unit mass. Potential energy is the sum of the gravitational potential energy relative to the geoid and the centripetal potential energy. (The geopotential is the specific potential energy.)'@en ;
    skos:notation 'SDN:P07::CFSNA033'^^xsd:string .
<feed> tree:member <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA034/1/> .
<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA034/1/>
    a skos:Concept ;
    dc:isVersionOf <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA034/>;
    dc:modified '2006-09-26T18:12:43'^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:altLabel ''^^xsd:string ;
    skos:prefLabel 'surface_downwelling_shortwave_flux'@en ;
    skos:definition 'The surface called \'surface\' means the lower boundary of the atmosphere. \'shortwave\' means shortwave radiation. Downwelling radiation is radiation from above. It does not mean \'net downward\'. Surface downwelling shortwave is the sum of direct and diffuse solar radiation incident on the surface, and is sometimes called \'global radiation\'. When thought of as being incident on a surface, a radiative flux is sometimes called \'irradiance\'. In addition, it is identical with the quantity measured by a cosine-collector light-meter and sometimes called \'vector irradiance\'. In accordance with common usage in geophysical disciplines, \'flux\' implies per unit area, called \'flux density\' in physics.'@en ;
    skos:notation 'SDN:P07::CFSNA034'^^xsd:string .
<feed> tree:member <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA035/1/> .
<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA035/1/>
    a skos:Concept ;
    dc:isVersionOf <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA035/>;
    dc:modified '2006-09-26T18:12:43'^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:altLabel ''^^xsd:string ;
    skos:prefLabel 'surface_downwelling_shortwave_flux_assuming_clear_sky'@en ;
    skos:definition 'The surface called \'surface\' means the lower boundary of the atmosphere. A phrase assuming_condition indicates that the named quantity is the value which would obtain if all aspects of the system were unaltered except for the assumption of the circumstances specified by the condition. \'shortwave\' means shortwave radiation. Downwelling radiation is radiation from above. It does not mean \'net downward\'. Surface downwelling shortwave is the sum of direct and diffuse solar radiation incident on the surface, and is sometimes called \'global radiation\'. When thought of as being incident on a surface, a radiative flux is sometimes called \'irradiance\'. In addition, it is identical with the quantity measured by a cosine-collector light-meter and sometimes called \'vector irradiance\'. In accordance with common usage in geophysical disciplines, \'flux\' implies per unit area, called \'flux density\' in physics.'@en ;
    skos:notation 'SDN:P07::CFSNA035'^^xsd:string .
<feed> tree:member <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSNA036/1/> .
